University Library Access Policy

This sample policy document explains borrowing privileges, library access, digital resource use, overdue materials, and conduct expectations. It is included as a public-style legal or policy document for the local RAG lab corpus. Library services support students, faculty, staff, visiting scholars, and approved community users.

Active students may borrow circulating books with a valid student card. Loan periods vary by item type. Reserve materials, reference books, archives, and special collections may have shorter access windows or reading-room-only restrictions. Users are responsible for checking due dates at the time of borrowing.

Digital databases are licensed for academic use. Users may download reasonable portions of articles or chapters for personal study, teaching, or research. Bulk downloading, automated scraping, credential sharing, and commercial redistribution are prohibited by license agreements and may result in suspended access.

Overdue notices are sent as a courtesy, but users remain responsible for returning items on time. Replacement fees may be charged for lost or damaged materials. A library hold may be placed on a student account when long-overdue items or unpaid replacement charges remain unresolved.

Quiet study zones require low noise and respectful behavior. Food and drinks may be restricted near computers, archives, media equipment, and special collections. Library staff may ask users to leave when behavior disrupts study, damages materials, or violates university rules.

Appeals for fines or replacement fees must be submitted to the circulation desk within thirty calendar days. The appeal should include the user ID, item title, due date, and any supporting evidence such as return receipts or communication records.

